// profile-greenthreads.test.maxon — GREEN THREADS ARE VISIBLE AS THEMSELVES, and the scheduler that runs
// them is not.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE CLAIM THIS CASE SETTLES.** A profiler that could only see OS threads would be nearly useless
// for a Maxon program: the scheduler multiplexes green threads onto a small pool of workers, so the fear is
// that every program is reported as spending its life inside the scheduler. The answer is structural rather
// than lucky — a context switch saves and restores the stack pointer AND the frame pointer, so an OS thread
// executing a green thread has that green thread's own stack in its registers, and sampling the worker IS
// sampling the task. Three consequences are asserted below:
//   1. `spinA` and `spinB` appear AS THEMSELVES, ranked by the work the fixture gives them;
//   2. they appear as TWO SEPARATE STACKS — each green thread's stack is its own allocation, so nothing had
//      to enumerate them, which matters because the only mechanism that CAN enumerate green threads is the
//      debug agent's, answered exclusively by a PARKED target, and parking a program once per sample is a
//      design this tree has already refused;
//   3. `__sched_worker_loop` appears NOWHERE — at any reporting floor, in any section, in any rendering.
//
// ⛔ **THAT ABSENCE IS ONLY WORTH ASSERTING BECAUSE THE SYMBOL COULD BE NAMED.** `__sched_worker_loop` is a
// real function in the emitted runtime and it is listed in the `.mxdbg` sidecar of this very fixture, so a
// profiler that attributed a worker's samples to the loop it was parked in would print it. The assertion is
// made over the WHOLE report rather than over the rows above a floor, because "nowhere at any floor" is the
// claim — a scheduler summarized into a "not shown" count is still a scheduler that was sampled.
//
// ⚠ **MAXON_MAX_PROCS IS PINNED, and an unpinned green-thread profile measures the machine as much as the
// program**: the scheduler decides its worker count at startup from that variable, so the number of workers
// with nothing to do — and therefore how much park/wake traffic the report carries — would otherwise be the
// host's answer rather than the fixture's. `--target-env` is the profiler's one way to set it, and the
// emitted runtime reads it through the environment at startup.
//
// ⚠ **THE 2:1 RATIO IS DELIBERATE.** With equal work the two rows sit inside each other's noise and their
// order is a coin flip; see `fixtures/greenthreads/main.maxon.fixture`.
//
// Staging, spawning, the folds applied to a captured stream, and the corpus's own reporting floor come from
// `ProfileHarness.maxon`.

// The two green threads the fixture spawns, and the order its work ratio makes a fact about the program.
let BusierTaskName = "spinA"
let QuieterTaskName = "spinB"

// ⭐ THE SYMBOL THAT MUST NOT BE NAMED. It is a real function in the emitted runtime and is listed in this
// fixture's own sidecar, which is what makes its absence a measurement rather than a spelling nobody could
// have produced.
let SchedulerWorkerLoopName = "__sched_worker_loop"

// How many worker processors the scheduler is pinned to, and how many stacks the fixture therefore runs: one
// per green thread. Two and not more — a third worker would add park/wake traffic that says nothing about
// the profiler.
let PinnedProcessorCount = "MAXON_MAX_PROCS=2"
let GreenThreadStackCount = 2

test 'two green threads are profiled as themselves and the scheduler is not'
	let staged = stagedProgram("profile-greenthreads", fixture: GreenThreadsFixtureName)
	buildProgram(staged, label: GreenThreadsFixtureName, extraArguments: StringArray.create())

	var pinned = StringArray.create()
	pinned.push("{TargetEnvFlagPrefix}{PinnedProcessorCount}")

	let run = runProfiler(staged, extraArguments: pinned)
	try Expect.equal(run.exitCode() as AssertedInt, expected: 0, message: "`{TestedCompilerStem} {ProfileSubcommandName} {ProfileRunVerb} {TargetEnvFlagPrefix}{PinnedProcessorCount}` did not complete a measurement of the green-thread fixture. `{TargetEnvFlagPrefix}` is the only way to pin the scheduler's worker count, and without it this profile measures the host as much as the program. It printed:{LineFeed}{run.stdout}{LineFeed}— and wrote to stderr: {run.stderr}")

	let lines = outputLines(run.stdout)
	try Expect.isFalse(reportHasLineStartingWith(lines, prefix: TooFewSamplesPrefix), message: "the profiler reported too few samples to rank anything, so the ordering of the two green threads below would be a coin flip. It printed:{LineFeed}{run.stdout}")
	try Expect.isTrue(reportHasLine(lines, wanted: HotFunctionsHeader), message: "the report carries no '{HotFunctionsHeader}' section, so nothing in it says which green thread ran. It printed:{LineFeed}{run.stdout}")
	try Expect.isTrue(reportHasLine(lines, wanted: CallTreeHeader), message: "the report carries no '{CallTreeHeader}' section. It printed:{LineFeed}{run.stdout}")
	try Expect.isTrue(reportHasLine(lines, wanted: StacksHeader), message: "the report carries no '{StacksHeader}' section, which is the only place the count of stacks that ran is stated. It printed:{LineFeed}{run.stdout}")

	let samples = totalSamples(run.stdout, profilerLabel: TestedCompilerStem)

	// ⭐ CLAIM 1 — the two tasks as themselves, ranked by the work the fixture gives them.
	let selfTimeNames = rowNames(significantRows(sectionRows(lines, header: HotFunctionsHeader), totalSamples: samples))
	try requireBothTasksRanked(selfTimeNames, section: HotFunctionsHeader, report: run.stdout)

	// The same two in the tree, where they are SIBLINGS: each hangs off the trampoline that started it, which
	// is what a green thread's stack genuinely looks like from the outside.
	let treeRows = significantRows(sectionRows(lines, header: CallTreeHeader), totalSamples: samples)
	let treeNames = rowNames(treeRows)
	try requireBothTasksRanked(treeNames, section: CallTreeHeader, report: run.stdout)

	let busierColumn = nameColumn(treeRowFor(treeRows, names: treeNames, wanted: BusierTaskName))
	let quieterColumn = nameColumn(treeRowFor(treeRows, names: treeNames, wanted: QuieterTaskName))
	try Expect.equal(busierColumn as AssertedInt, expected: quieterColumn as AssertedInt, message: "the call tree indents `{BusierTaskName}` and `{QuieterTaskName}` differently, though neither called the other: two green threads are siblings under the frame that spawned them. A difference here means one task's stack was walked into the other's. It printed:{LineFeed}{renderedRows(treeRows)}")

	// ⭐ CLAIM 2 — two stacks, because two green threads ran. Nothing enumerated them; their stack allocations
	// did.
	let stackNames = rowNames(significantRows(sectionRows(lines, header: StacksHeader), totalSamples: samples))
	try Expect.equal(stackNames.count() as AssertedInt, expected: GreenThreadStackCount, message: "the report lists {stackNames.count()} stack(s) above this corpus's floor, and the fixture runs {GreenThreadStackCount} green threads on {PinnedProcessorCount}. ONE would mean both tasks were folded onto the worker that happened to run them, which is the whole failure this case exists to catch. It listed:{LineFeed}{renderedRows(stackNames)}")

	// ⭐⭐ CLAIM 3 — and the scheduler is nowhere, at any floor, in any section. Asserted over the WHOLE report
	// rather than over the rows above a floor: a scheduler summarized into a "not shown" count is still a
	// scheduler that was sampled.
	try Expect.isFalse(withoutCarriageReturns(run.stdout).contains(SchedulerWorkerLoopName), message: "`{SchedulerWorkerLoopName}` appears in the profile. It is the loop a worker runs BETWEEN green threads, and a sampler that attributed a task's samples to it would report every Maxon program as spending its life in the scheduler — burying the work under the machinery that dispatched it. The symbol is in this fixture's own sidecar, so naming it is something the profiler can do and must not. It printed:{LineFeed}{run.stdout}")
end 'two green threads are profiled as themselves and the scheduler is not'

// Both tasks present, and the busier one first — asked of two sections, so it is written once.
function requireBothTasksRanked(names StringArray, section String, report String) throws TestFailure
	try Expect.isTrue(names.contains(BusierTaskName), message: "'{section}' does not list `{BusierTaskName}`, a green thread that spends the run doing arithmetic. A profiler that saw only OS threads would name the worker that ran it instead. It listed:{LineFeed}{renderedRows(names)}{LineFeed}The whole report was:{LineFeed}{report}")
	try Expect.isTrue(names.contains(QuieterTaskName), message: "'{section}' does not list `{QuieterTaskName}`, the second green thread — half the work of `{BusierTaskName}`, which is several times this corpus's floor rather than a rounding error. A report naming only one of two tasks is one that folded them together. It listed:{LineFeed}{renderedRows(names)}{LineFeed}The whole report was:{LineFeed}{report}")
	let busierPosition = positionOf(names, wanted: BusierTaskName)
	let quieterPosition = positionOf(names, wanted: QuieterTaskName)
	try Expect.lessThan(busierPosition as AssertedInt, than: quieterPosition as AssertedInt, message: "'{section}' ranks `{QuieterTaskName}` above `{BusierTaskName}`, though the fixture gives `{BusierTaskName}` twice the work. The ratio is deliberate: with equal work this ordering would be a coin flip, and with it the ordering is a fact about the program. It listed:{LineFeed}{renderedRows(names)}")
end 'requireBothTasksRanked'

// The call-tree row a name was found on. The name's presence has already been asserted, so the lookup cannot
// fail.
function treeRowFor(rows StringArray, names StringArray, wanted String) returns String
	return try rows.get(positionOf(names, wanted: wanted)) otherwise panic("profile-greenthreads.test: the gate could not run — '{wanted}' was found among {names.count()} names taken from these {rows.count()} rows.")
end 'treeRowFor'
